.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/follow-redirects Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Follow Redirects"
.PP
Drop-in replacement for Node's \fRhttp\fP and \fRhttps\fP modules that automatically follows redirects\&.

.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
\fRfollow-redirects\fP provides \fRrequest\fP and \fRget\fP methods that behave identically to those found on the native \fRhttp\fP and \fRhttps\fP modules, with the exception that they will seamlessly follow redirects\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { http, https } = require('follow\-redirects');

http\&.get('http://bit\&.ly/900913', response => {
  response\&.on('data', chunk => {
    console\&.log(chunk);
  });
})\&.on('error', err => {
  console\&.error(err);
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can inspect the final redirected URL through the \fRresponseUrl\fP property on the \fRresponse\fP\&. If no redirection happened, \fRresponseUrl\fP is the original request URL\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const request = https\&.request({
  host: 'bitly\&.com',
  path: '/UHfDGO',
}, response => {
  console\&.log(response\&.responseUrl);
  // 'http://duckduckgo\&.com/robots\&.txt'
});
request\&.end();
.fi
.PP
.SH "Options"
.PP
.SS "Global options"
Global options are set directly on the \fRfollow-redirects\fP module:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const followRedirects = require('follow\-redirects');
followRedirects\&.maxRedirects = 10;
followRedirects\&.maxBodyLength = 20 * 1024 * 1024; // 20 MB
.fi
.PP

.PP
The following global options are supported:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxRedirects\fP (default: \fR21\fP) – sets the maximum number of allowed redirects; if exceeded, an error will be emitted\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxBodyLength\fP (default: 10MB) – sets the maximum size of the request body; if exceeded, an error will be emitted\&.
.PP
.SS "Per-request options"
Per-request options are set by passing an \fRoptions\fP object:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const url = require('url');
const { http, https } = require('follow\-redirects');

const options = url\&.parse('http://bit\&.ly/900913');
options\&.maxRedirects = 10;
options\&.beforeRedirect = (options, response, request) => {
  // Use this to adjust the request options upon redirecting,
  // to inspect the latest response headers,
  // or to cancel the request by throwing an error

  // response\&.headers = the redirect response headers
  // response\&.statusCode = the redirect response code (eg\&. 301, 307, etc\&.)

  // request\&.url = the requested URL that resulted in a redirect
  // request\&.headers = the headers in the request that resulted in a redirect
  // request\&.method = the method of the request that resulted in a redirect
  if (options\&.hostname === "example\&.com") {
    options\&.auth = "user:password";
  }
};
http\&.request(options);
.fi
.PP

.PP
In addition to the \fRstandard HTTP\fP and \fRHTTPS options\fP, the following per-request options are supported:
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRfollowRedirects\fP (default: \fRtrue\fP) – whether redirects should be followed\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxRedirects\fP (default: \fR21\fP) – sets the maximum number of allowed redirects; if exceeded, an error will be emitted\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxBodyLength\fP (default: 10MB) – sets the maximum size of the request body; if exceeded, an error will be emitted\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbeforeRedirect\fP (default: \fRundefined\fP) – optionally change the request \fRoptions\fP on redirects, or abort the request by throwing an error\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRagents\fP (default: \fRundefined\fP) – sets the \fRagent\fP option per protocol, since HTTP and HTTPS use different agents\&. Example value: \fR{ http: new http\&.Agent(), https: new https\&.Agent() }\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtrackRedirects\fP (default: \fRfalse\fP) – whether to store the redirected response details into the \fRredirects\fP array on the response object\&.
.PP
.SS "Advanced usage"
By default, \fRfollow-redirects\fP will use the Node\&.js default implementations of \fR\fRhttp\fP\fP and \fR\fRhttps\fP\fP\&. To enable features such as caching and/or intermediate request tracking, you might instead want to wrap \fRfollow-redirects\fP around custom protocol implementations:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { http, https } = require('follow\-redirects')\&.wrap({
  http: require('your\-custom\-http'),
  https: require('your\-custom\-https'),
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
Such custom protocols only need an implementation of the \fRrequest\fP method\&.
.SH "Browser Usage"
.PP
Due to the way the browser works, the \fRhttp\fP and \fRhttps\fP browser equivalents perform redirects by default\&.

.PP
By requiring \fRfollow-redirects\fP this way: 
.PP
.nf
const http = require('follow\-redirects/http');
const https = require('follow\-redirects/https');

.fi
.PP
 you can easily tell webpack and friends to replace \fRfollow-redirect\fP by the built-in versions:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "follow\-redirects/http"  : "http",
  "follow\-redirects/https" : "https"
}
.fi
.PP
.SH "Contributing"
.PP
Pull Requests are always welcome\&. Please \fRfile an issue\fP detailing your proposal before you invest your valuable time\&. Additional features and bug fixes should be accompanied by tests\&. You can run the test suite locally with a simple \fRnpm test\fP command\&.
.SH "Debug Logging"
.PP
\fRfollow-redirects\fP uses the excellent \fRdebug\fP for logging\&. To turn on logging set the environment variable \fRDEBUG=follow-redirects\fP for debug output from just this module\&. When running the test suite it is sometimes advantageous to set \fRDEBUG=*\fP to see output from the express server as well\&.
